---
import { Code } from "astro:components";

import ComponentPage from "../../layouts/ComponentPage.astro";
import SeparatorDemo from "../../components/SeparatorDemo";
import { codeTheme } from "../../components/code-theme";

const usageExample = `import { Separator } from "@/components/ui/separator";

<section>…</section>
<Separator />
<section>…</section>

<nav className="flex items-center gap-3">
  <a href="/docs">Docs</a>
  <Separator orientation="vertical" decorative />
  <a href="/blog">Blog</a>
</nav>`;
---

<ComponentPage slug="separator">
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    Horizontal by default, as a native <code>&lt;hr&gt;</code>.
  </Fragment>

  <SeparatorDemo slot="demo" client:visible />

  <Fragment slot="usage">
    <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Usage</h2>
    <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm [&_pre]:p-4">
      <Code code={usageExample} lang="tsx" theme={codeTheme} />
    </div>
    <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-muted-foreground">
      <li>
        <strong>Semantic.</strong> A horizontal separator is an <code>&lt;hr&gt;</code>, which
        screen readers announce as a separator between sections. A vertical one is a
        <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> with <code>role="separator"</code> and
        <code>aria-orientation="vertical"</code>. Neither is focusable.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Decorative.</strong> When the line only decorates, such as between links that are
        already separate items, pass <code>decorative</code>. It renders <code>role="none"</code>{" "}
        with <code>aria-hidden</code>, so screen readers skip it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Vertical in a row.</strong> The vertical line stretches to the height of its flex
        row; give the row a height or let its content set one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Forced colors.</strong> The line is a border, not a background, so it stays visible
        in forced-colors mode.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Color</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
      A separator that marks structure is a graphic people need to see, so it should reach 3:1
      against the page. It uses the <code>border</code> color, the token meant for dividers. In the
      Buril theme that is ink, far above 3:1, which matches the engraved grid lines. For a quieter
      line, pass <code>className="border-input"</code>: Buril measures it at 3.72:1 in light mode
      and 3.35:1 in dark mode. Lighter mixes fall below 3:1, so keep them for decorative lines.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <p slot="install-notes" class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
    It creates <code>components/ui/separator.tsx</code>, the same path and props as shadcn's
    Separator, without Radix: the horizontal one is a native <code>&lt;hr&gt;</code>. It needs only
    your <code>cn()</code> helper; if your <code>lib/utils.ts</code> differs from the current shadcn
    version, the CLI asks before replacing it, and answering no is safe.
  </p>
</ComponentPage>
